{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And the cloud covered the Tent of Meeting.</b> So it is written at Mount Sinai (Exodus 24:15): ״ויכס הענן את ההר״, and the Tent of Meeting was covered from every side by the cloud. And it says: וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן — for inside the Tabernacle the glory of YHWH filled it, since the glory dwells within the cloud, inside the Tabernacle; for thus it is written at Mount Sinai (Exodus 20): ״ומשה נגש אל הערפל אשר שם האלהים״ — because the cloud rested upon it, Scripture says that because the cloud rested upon it, therefore Moses was not able to enter the Tent of Meeting — that is, without a call; but with a call he was permitted to enter there. And not that the call 'Moses, Moses' would be for him permission to enter while the glory was still there, but rather that it would be for him a sign that the flow of the light of the glory from the Tabernacle had already been completed, apart from the cherubim, and with this Moses was permitted to enter the Tabernacle. And so we find at Mount Sinai (Exodus 24): ״ויקרא אל משה ביום השביעי מתוך הענן״, and it is written (there): ״ויבא משה בתוך הענן״. And this is why it was necessary to write ויקרא אל משה in immediate proximity — for everything is bound and joined together; even though the books are separate, all is one bond, a man with his brother they shall be joined, interlaced, and not separated." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> It is possible that the verse, having said once, ״וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן״, alludes to the glory that dwells within it. Thus far his words, of blessed memory." "<b>And I hereby rouse you</b> to note that there are five verses here, and in each and every verse 'cloud' is mentioned, and they correspond to the five sefirot, which are the five voices that the Sages referred to in 'the Torah was given with five voices.' And even though Moses grasped seven voices, which are seven sefirot — as I explained in the portion of וישמע יתרו — their intention in saying 'with five voices' was to the hidden voices, which are five. Therefore they said, 'the Torah was given with five voices,' for the two that are Tiferet and Kavod are explicit in Scripture, namely ה׳ אלהים, and this is ה׳ אלהיך. Corresponding to them, 'וכבוד ה׳' is mentioned here twice, in order to allude to Tiferet dwelling within it — Kavod. And understand this, for to this the Rav, of blessed memory, intended in his language that I cited: that he went back and mentioned a second time וכבוד ה׳. And so we find in the Temple, as it is written in Chronicles (II 7): ״וככלות שלמה להתפלל והאש ירדה מן השמים ותאכל העולה והזבחים וכבוד ה׳ מלא את הבית״, ״ולא יכלו הכהנים לבא אל בית ה׳ כי מלא כבוד ה׳ את בית ה׳״. From this you learn that seven sefirot are mentioned here: five times 'cloud,' two times 'וכבוד ה׳' — for thus were alluded to at Mount Sinai seven voices, which were the grasp of Moses, as I explained there."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:08:15.126137387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}